    "content": "Brigadier General Tariq Saleh, a prominent Yemeni military figure and member of the Presidential Leadership Council, has reportedly fled Yemen by sea to Somaliland following the withdrawal of his forces from the coastal city of Al Mukha. The Houthi movement seized control of Al Mukha on Thursday after Saleh's National Resistance forces pulled out, according to reports. Saleh is said to have arrived in Hargeisa, the capital of Somaliland, though neither Somaliland authorities nor Yemen's government have independently verified this information. The reports emerged on Saturday, citing The Daily Somalia.\n\nSaleh commands the National Resistance, a force loyal to the Yemeni government, and has been a key player in the conflict against the Houthis on Yemen's western coast. His departure from Al Mukha marks a significant setback for the anti-Houthi coalition, which has been battling the Iran-aligned group since 2014. The Houthis' takeover of Al Mukha, a strategic port city on the Red Sea, further consolidates their control over northern Yemen and poses a threat to international shipping lanes. Saleh's reported flight to Somaliland, a self-declared independent region in northern Somalia, raises questions about his future role in the conflict and the broader regional dynamics.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is particularly significant due to Somaliland's proximity to Yemen and its strategic location along the Gulf of Aden. The presence of a high-profile Yemeni military leader in Hargeisa could strain Somaliland's delicate diplomatic position, as it seeks international recognition while balancing relations with Yemen, the Gulf states, and Somalia. The diaspora has long been concerned about the spillover of Yemen's conflict into the Horn of Africa, including the risk of Houthi attacks on shipping and potential refugee flows. Saleh's arrival may also prompt discussions within the diaspora about Somaliland's role in regional security and its ties to Yemeni factions.\n\nThe Houthi takeover of Al Mukha follows years of shifting frontline dynamics in Yemen's civil war. Saleh, a nephew of former Yemeni President Ali Abdullah Saleh, has been a controversial figure, having previously fought alongside the Houthis before aligning with the Saudi-led coalition. His forces have received support from the United Arab Emirates and have been instrumental in securing the western coast. The loss of Al Mukha could weaken the anti-Houthi resistance and embolden the Houthis, who have recently escalated attacks on shipping in the Red Sea, disrupting global trade. The reported move to Somaliland, if confirmed, would mark a new chapter in Saleh's political and military career, possibly involving exile and continued resistance from abroad.\n\nSomaliland, which declared independence in 1991 but remains unrecognized internationally, has generally maintained a neutral stance in Yemen's conflict. However, its strategic port of Berbera and its proximity to Yemen have made it a focal point for regional powers. The Somali diaspora, which maintains strong ties to Somaliland, will be watching closely for official statements from Hargeisa and any implications for local security. The lack of confirmation from Somaliland or Yemeni officials leaves many questions unanswered, including Saleh's exact status and whether his presence is temporary or long-term. The diaspora's concern stems from potential instability that could affect remittances, trade, and family ties across the region.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/yemen-tariq-saleh-flees-somaliland-houthi-takeover-al-mukha/",
