    "summary": "President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud met with Dr. Mohammed bin Abdulkarim Al-Issa at the presidential palace to discuss strengthening Somalia-MWL cooperation, focusing on countering extremism and promoting peace.",
    "content": "Somali President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud received Dr. Mohammed bin Abdulkarim Al-Issa, Secretary General of the Muslim World League (MWL) and Chairman of the Organization of Muslim Scholars, at the National Presidential Palace in Mogadishu on Thursday. The meeting centered on deepening ties and cooperation between Somalia and the MWL, with particular attention to promoting a correct understanding of Islam, preventing extremist ideologies, and fostering peace and communal coexistence.\n\nDuring the talks, President Mohamud emphasized the importance of collaborative efforts to counter extremist narratives and encouraged the MWL to support Somalia's ongoing stabilization and reconciliation initiatives. Dr. Al-Issa reaffirmed the MWL's commitment to advancing moderate Islamic values and supporting peace-building projects in the region. The discussions also touched on the role of religious scholars in countering radicalization.\n\nThe Muslim World League, based in Mecca, Saudi Arabia, is an international non-governmental organization founded in 1962. It engages in humanitarian, educational, and interfaith activities across the globe. Dr. Al-Issa, a former Saudi justice minister, has led the organization since 2016 and has been actively promoting interfaith dialogue and countering extremism worldwide. This visit is part of the MWL's broader outreach to Muslim-majority nations facing security challenges.\n\nSomalia has been battling Al-Shabaab, an al-Qaeda-linked militant group, for over a decade. The government has recently intensified military operations against the group, which has carried out numerous attacks in Somalia and neighboring countries. The meeting underscores the government's focus on ideological countermeasures alongside military action. It also highlights the importance of international partnerships in addressing the drivers of extremism.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this engagement represents a significant step in global efforts to combat extremism that often affects their communities abroad. Diaspora members have long been targets of radical recruitment, and initiatives like this could lead to more resources for deradicalization and community resilience programs. Additionally, the partnership with the MWL may open avenues for diaspora scholars and organizations to participate in peace-building efforts back home.\n\nThe meeting concluded with both parties agreeing to explore further avenues of cooperation, including educational exchanges and joint programs to promote moderate Islam and counter extremist propaganda. No specific agreements were announced, but the dialogue signals a mutual interest in sustained engagement.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/12/madaxweyne-xasan-sheekh-oo-xoghayaha-guud-ee-raabidada-caalamka-islaamka-kala-hadlay-la-dagaallanka-xagjirnimada/",
