    "content": "Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi held talks with Iranian President Masoud Pezeshkian in New Delhi on Friday, focusing on maritime security, freedom of navigation, and the protection of seafarers. The meeting took place ahead of the BRICS leaders' summit and comes amid rising concerns over attacks on commercial vessels in the region.\n\nAccording to Indian officials, Modi stressed the importance of keeping sea lanes open and ensuring the safety of ships and their crews to prevent disruptions to global trade. The discussions also covered recent attacks that have heightened risks for vessels operating in key waterways.\n\nThis engagement follows a series of incidents in the region that have threatened shipping routes, prompting India to advocate for enhanced maritime cooperation. Iran, a key player in the region, shares an interest in maintaining stability in the waters that connect major trading nations.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, many of whom rely on remittances from relatives working in the Gulf and beyond, any disruption to global shipping can affect the cost and availability of goods, as well as the livelihoods of seafarers. The safety of maritime corridors is also critical for humanitarian supplies reaching Somalia.\n\nThe talks between India and Iran underscore the growing diplomatic efforts to address maritime security challenges. Both leaders agreed to continue coordination on issues affecting regional and international shipping.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/12/hindiya-iyo-iiraan-oo-ka-wada-hadlay-badbaadada-maraakiibta-iyo-badmaaxiinta-hindiya/",
